+// Dawn calls the store callback once per blob it produces -- several per
+// pipeline -- and this used to open, journal and commit a separate SQLite
+// transaction for each one. On Horizon that is a rollback-journal create,
+// two fsync-equivalents and a journal delete against the SD card, per blob,
+// on the shader-compilation thread. With a 6,000-entry pipeline seed to warm
+// it is thousands of SD-card commits competing with the game's own file I/O,
+// and it is why enabling this cache at all made the port slower than leaving
+// it broken.
+//
+// Buffer the blobs and commit them in batches instead. Reads consult the
+// buffer first so a store followed immediately by a load still hits -- Dawn
+// does exactly that for shader modules shared between pipelines, and missing
+// there would cost a recompile, which is far more expensive than the scan.
+struct PendingCacheWrite {
+  XXH128_hash_t key;
+  std::vector<uint8_t> stored;  // possibly zstd-compressed
+  uint64_t originalSize = 0;
+  int compressed = 0;
